<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/75" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**2. :fas-info-circle: [Discussion] Most Optimal Parsers Design**
</div>

Continuing from #69, I realised that many people have different methods for parsing each command. I was wondering (Copied and pasted from my replies in that issue **as I feel like this issue itself should have its own discussion thread)**: 

Note that everything here is discussed in terms of OOP design!

If Parser happens to be responsible to parse our commands, would it be more efficient to have a Parser functional interface? (i.e., interface Paser&lt;T extends Command>) w/ a T parse(String input). So now, each command that needs parsing can have an CommandParser class and those without, you can just put into the HashMap using an anonymous class as it just returns T (the command) if you just need to run Command#run.

I would like to expand discussion on this whether it's actually a good idea. At the top of my head, it feels like it is a very good design.

Let's say Map&lt;String, Parser&lt;? extends Command>> ...., I can just do args -&gt; ByeCommand or for a command that needs parsing, new EventParserCommand() - that extends the Parser interface.

Your main parser class can then focus on feeding these split args into these commands as needed. Cleaner code, main parser class does a central responsibility and nothing more.

This way, we can also have stateful commands (going back to the previously talked about Map&lt;String, CommandFactory> because the Parser class is always returning a new instance of a Command class! If DeleteCommand is ever implemented, we can hold the index of it and undo if ever needed without sharing the same instance as before!

I think this eliminates the need for multiple methods e.g., parseEvent, parseXXXX in your Parser.java just to split the method up to make it shorter. Also makes it look cleaner and easier to read like the polymorphism for Commands; instead of scrolling a Parser.java file full of methods doing multiple things for multiple commands. 

P.S., if too complicated, u can also do a Parser functional interface that returns Command. I thought of generics because it helps with JUnit testings, e.g., if you want to test for an EventParserCommand, you can limit the Parser to only accept EventCommands and in a developer's standpoint, it forces other developer to narrow down their method to return a specific command instead of a generic command.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/69" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**3. :fas-info-circle: 💡Tips on avoiding long method body & adhering to SLAP/OOP**
</div>

I wanted to share this as how it's always very important to think of good OOP design when designing software applications. As we build up our iP and subsequently our tP next, one pattern that almost most of us would start with is using a giant `if-else` or `switch` statement for Command Parsing.

e.g., 

''' java
switch (command) ``{``
   case "deadline":
    // ....
``}``
'''

As you can see, as the increments go by, this body of code grows longer each increment and no longer adheres to SLAP, "no very long methods". Your main code will also be very hard to read and debug.

A better way would be making use of what we learnt in 2030S, by combining polymorphism and hashmaps that can dynamically search up for the user's commands when it's parsed. I won't give the full answer here, but feel free to discuss on what you think the design would be! I'll be answering your questions and helping you along the way if necessary. 

If you do it right, by the end of week 3's increments, you should have a beautiful folder full of command classes :)

With this design, we can also work better in teams as encapsulation becomes clearer and cleaner as teammates can develop isolated commands independently with minimal **merge** conflicts.

The marking rubrics states this
> Reasonable use of SLAP, e.g., no very long methods or deeply nested code.

</panel>

