> Isn't this a long `switch` VS a long string-to-function hash map? At somewhere some point the long pattern matching block must exist - I'm guessing the most we can do is (if I were to clean it up), a `switch` expression where each branch is hopefully a one-liner consisting of just a function call. Or transfer that block into a mapping ("registry"), then that makes it so that a long _method_ does not exist (but something else that's not a method but is long, exists).
> 
> (Just throwing out a thought, not making a position here.)
> 
> Edit: thanks for starting this discussion, reminds me of further cleaning up iP code. Just spotted a few places deserving some cleanup.

You’re right to say that. However, in terms of OOP, I would argue that even if your switch expression consists a one liner `case “deadline” -&gt; deadlineCommand()`, this would violate OOP despite adhering to SLAP with a shorter method body.

As your switch statement is most likely to reside in your main `Bot.java` class, this class should only handle the overall flow of the chatbot (i.e., read input -&gt; get response and should not know the overarching structure of commands as per OOP. 

Individual command classes encapsulates their own exact algorithm and is a cleaner design in my opinion.

</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/69#issuecomment-5434553698" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**16 :fas-comment:**
</div>

> Haven't tested if this works, but I am considering using something like this to dynamically load the relevant Command class and execute it: `Class.forName(className)` (Some string transformation probably can be done to convert `command` to PascalCase for `className`)
> 
> Then the object can be casted to a parent class or interface at runtime, with each class overriding method that executes the command.
> 
> I think this might be a good solution to reduce the need to both create the class and add the switch case as the only thing we will need to change for each new command is the class's Java file itself.

Nice catch, but I think reflections is a bit of an overkill! Also, dynamically loading via class names can introduce a lot of accidents, especially working in a team environment. Also makes debugging difficult because these strings are linked to class names and the paths of them might not be clear. 



> I think command classes are useful, but reflection may be overkill here. A short `switch` or a `Map&lt;String, CommandFactory>` can handle parsing, while each `Command` class contains its own execution logic.
> 
> The map may still be long, but it is just a list of mappings rather than complex control flow. `Class.forName()` also introduces runtime errors if a class is renamed or has the wrong constructor. For an iP with a fixed set of commands, an explicit switch or map seems simpler and easier to test.

Good catch! You're right, we can use `Map&lt;String, CommandFactory>` if our commands are stateful (i.e., a DeleteCommand that could be undo'd down the road). However, for stateless commands, we can also use `Map&lt;String, Command>` that uses the same command instances throughout!
</panel>

