<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/73#issuecomment-5435245734" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**16 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Before Level 8 I stored deadline and event dates as plain strings, so my save file happily contained lines like:                                           

D | 0 | submit report | next Friday

After switching to LocalDateTime, those lines obviously can't be parsed any more. The way my code deals with this is that the loader parses the save file one line at a time, with each line wrapped in its own try/catch. When a line's date field fails to parse, it prints a message saying it couldn't read that particular line, then carries on with the remaining lines instead of giving up on the file. So the legacy tasks are dropped, but every other task still loads and start-up never crashes.

A nice side effect is that this isn't specific to the date change at all — the same handling covers general data corruption, since an unrecognised task type or a line with the wrong number of fields throws through the same path and gets skipped the same way.

One detail I only noticed afterwards: because my save routine rewrites the whole file from the in-memory list after every command that changes the list, the skipped lines are permanently gone as soon as you type your next command.

</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/94#issuecomment-5452041943" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**17 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I did this:
1. Merge the increment branch back to master with NO FAST FORWARDING
2. Tag the merge commit
3. Push master branch and tag
4. Push increment branch

as per instructions from https://nus-cs2103-ay2627-s1.github.io/website/schedule/week3/project.html:

> Start a branch branch-Level-7.
> Implement Level 7 while committing to that branch at appropriate points.
> Merge the branch back to the master branch (remember to create a merge commit, i.e., no fast-forward).
> Git tag the merge commit in the master branch as usual (i.e., add the tag Level-7).
> Push the following three things to your fork:
> (a) the master branch,
> (b) the branch-Level-7 branch,
> (c) the Level-7 tag.
</panel>

