<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/116#issuecomment-5483471696" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**22 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I think the main difference is:
greenfield:
1. You start from scratch (or basically scratch in the iP as you are just provided the skeleton code)
2. You have to decide on the architecture and how to implement the code from requirements
3. Dont need to maintain any older code (dont have to check for regressions in older code).

brownfield:
1. You have to understand the existing codebase before you change it
2. Architecture is given which means that the code already implements the requirements (although might not be a good implementation)
3. You should follow the existing coding convention and standards as per CS2103T advice
4. Make sure your additions do not cause any regressions in the older code.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/112#issuecomment-5483632340" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**23 :fas-comment:**
</div>

One downside of using AI to assist in coding is that it is very tempting and easy to let the AI do all the coding by itself while you simply accept the changes. This means once you stop understanding the code, which causes you to lose the ability to review the code AI write, which also leads to you just accepting the changes and depending on AI even more. This causes a vicious cycle.

Using AI also doesnt mean that you dont need to understand or review the code. If some earlier commits AI generated contains error, you most likely have to read through the entire codebase to understand it before coming with a fix.

However, if you are disciplined enough, AI can really improve your productivity. For example, you can get AI to find bugs that might otherwise take you hours to track down manually.
</panel>

</panel>


