<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/75#issuecomment-5450445795" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**11 :fas-comment:**
</div>

> In terms of git and what this mod is teaching, I feel like it would still be a good design. This is because if a teammate is implementing a new command, they only need to create their CommandParser and register it, without modifying a shared, ever-scaling, switch block where merge conflict would likely happen.

Well, there will still have to be a singular source of command handling, so you just shift that from a switch case to a map, so I don't entirely agree that this is the main benefit. e.g. consider the AB3 current command handling:

'''java
        return switch (commandWord) ``{``
            case AddCommand.COMMAND_WORD -&gt; new AddCommandParser().parse(arguments);
            case EditCommand.COMMAND_WORD -&gt; new EditCommandParser().parse(arguments);
            case DeleteCommand.COMMAND_WORD -&gt; new DeleteCommandParser().parse(arguments);
            case ClearCommand.COMMAND_WORD -&gt; new ClearCommand();
            case FindCommand.COMMAND_WORD -&gt; new FindCommandParser().parse(arguments);
            case ListCommand.COMMAND_WORD -&gt; new ListCommand();
            case ExitCommand.COMMAND_WORD -&gt; new ExitCommand();
            case HelpCommand.COMMAND_WORD -&gt; new HelpCommand();
            default -&gt; ``{``
                logger.finer("This user input caused a ParseException: " + userInput);
                throw new ParseException(MESSAGE_UNKNOWN_COMMAND);
            ``}``
        ``}``;
'''

In this respect, I believe a map-vs-switch statement choice is really just a stylistic preference (data-driven vs code-driven dispatch).

> By defining `Parser&lt;T extends Command>`, the interface explicitly forces subclasses (e.g., `TodoCommandParser implements Parser&lt;TodoCommand>`) to specify & return that exact command type. So this reduces ambiguity and guarantees that the parser produces the intended command, and could make JUnit testing much cleaner without unnecessary castings.

But I do agree that the generics that you brought up does make testing a lot cleaner, although it doesn't really explicitly force subclasses to specify and return that exact command type, because someone could really just do `class TodoCommandParser implements Parser&lt;Command>` and nothing would be stopping them from doing it, it just makes it expressible I guess. But I would think that the cleaner testing is a good reason to keep the generic.

Overall, I wouldn't call the switch statement a bad design necessarily, just personal stylistic preference.
</panel>

