</div>

<img width="492" height="261" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/69655235-df42-4954-9d5d-53cdda81548f" />

In this course the iP is treated as a greenfield project while the tP is treated as a brownfield project, as covered in the[ week 2 briefing video](https://nus-cs2103-ay2627-s1.github.io/website/schedule/week2/index.html#:~:text=1.%20Iterative%20%2B%20Brownfield%20%3D%20%3F%3F%3F%3A%20How%20the%20iterative%20and%20brownfield%20approach%20used%20in%20the%20course%20affects%20your%20learning%20experience.).

If I recall correctly, it was mentioned in the [Week 2 -&gt; 3 briefing](https://nus-sg.zoom.us/rec/share/hUC5QsDQeonis9Y_3ASnn81bG7ZAcGMoqfz1_P2Zo4DVeBi9BBhbNFne1WlRFFih.avpTABpUjAviLa_c?pwd=DHp69RkTCmRCKp1MEwAAIAAAAKLBWDXKuKoZ6jGjL478HlZDEhjgmOGL53hFX1z8t42-X9QOt4qmkBGjKVD8_49-HTAwMDAwNA) (around the 49:30 mark) that despite giving some code to start with, iP is still considered a greenfield project because there is no actual product that was given to you.

I think if there is no tangible product that was given to you to work on, you can consider it as a greenfield project as you are adding most if not all of the features yourself. However, all of this is subjective, and I would like to hear others' opinions on this as well.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/119#issuecomment-5488877131" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**16 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I think as long as the intermediate state where the Task is not belonging to any list is "hidden" in your implementation of move (ie the system can never access this state), then it does not violate the multiplicity. 

For example in your point about moving a list, if your code chooses to do so by first removing and adding, as long as both `remove()` and `add()` are encapsulated in your `move` function, then the system will never know what is happening internally when  you call the `move` function, it will only see that the task has moved to a new list but the multiplicity is still maintained. 

However, if the `remove()` and `add()`  are two different functions in your code, there will be an **accessible** state where Task is not belonging to any list. For example when trying to move a task, your code first did `remove(task)`, but before it could add add it back to the next list your system runs `printlist()` and notices that the task is now missing. This will violate the multiplicity.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/120#issuecomment-5490273818" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**17 :fas-comment:**
</div>

