I think having TODO comments in the commits depends on the situation. 

Since a commit is visible by everyone and especially next time in the workplace, if you did decide to do a TODO in a commit
You OUGHT TO indicate in future commits when you have marked this todo as resolved just to make it very clear.

Eg in a future git commit message body:
Implement XXXX feature from commit ID

It is sort of sub-optimal for reasons that a commit is historical in nature and you dont want to tell others "hey theres this I need to fix".
I personally also have some TODOs in my previous git commits. No right or wrong its your own practice so long as you make sure all ends are properly tied regardless of whatever you prefer.

But I guess a better practice is to maintain a TODO file instead.
Just create a new plaintext file, do .gitignore it and then use it to track your status.
[Or you can use your current iP to create a TODO tracking bot 🤔 😆 ]

Anyways heres a stackoverflow thread regarding this: https://stackoverflow.com/questions/51260140/is-it-a-good-habit-to-write-todo-to-commit-message

Cheers!


</panel>

