<div slot="header">

**6 :fas-comment:**
</div>

> Correct me if I'm wrong, but from my understanding, token usage can accumulate easily because the model is repeatedly sent the context containing previous conversation history.

While technically true (transformer architecture is inherently O(n&hat;2)), in practice inference costs (as in billing, at least) are closer to linear due to KV caching.

But if really necessary, for cost management, GPT-5.6 Luna at Max reasoning is [anecdotally](https://artificialanalysis.ai/evaluations/artificial-analysis-intelligence-index?eval-cost=intelligence-vs-cost-per-task#cost) way cheaper for a "good enough" / "competitive" intelligence. Though I have been running Sol xhigh for my iP at [AI-4 to AI-5](https://nus-cs2103-ay2627-s1.github.io/website/admin/courseExpectations.html#use-of-ai) and it barely makes a dent.

For OpenCode, you could try the Ox Alpha model, it is completely free right now (for a limited time) and allegedly [citation needed] performs on par with Sol.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/76#issuecomment-5449592978" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**7 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Ultimately the question comes down to what `TaskList` does in your codebase -- if it is literally just a list purpose-made for `Task`s then sure why not, though then the question becomes why a separate class instead of just e.g. `ArrayList&lt;Task>`.

As per LSP, this `TaskList` would need to work in all aspects one could reasonably find `ArrayList&lt;Task>`. For example, are you encapsulating error handling, i.e. would you want `TaskList` to still throw your custom exception for negative usage? Are you trying to improve (restrict) the abstraction barrier? Are you bundling storage logic? Any such decision would immediately (in my opinion) favour composition over inheritance. Yes the project is still small at this point, so it doesn't really matter, but more likely how you envision the idea of "TaskList" to be in the context of potential future extensibility would be the decisive factor in this.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/87#issuecomment-5449919576" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**8 :fas-comment:**
</div>

The style for "multiple statements" is specified [here](https://se-education.org/guides/conventions/java/index.html#layout):

'''java
switch (condition) ``{``
    case ABC:
        statements;
        // Fallthrough
    case DEF:
        statements;
        break;
    case XYZ:
        statements;
        break;
    default:
        statements;
        break;
``}``
'''

Arguably that doesn't specify that you _can't_ use the `-> ``{````}``` notation. But to my understanding, since they are equivalent representations (in fact `:` is strictly more expressive than `-> ``{````}```) it would be reasonable to assume that the "rest of the codebase" uses the `:` style and you should probably stick with that.

(But of course if an authoritative figure would like to comment on this then that would be ideal)
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/87#issuecomment-5454566457" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**9 :fas-comment:**
</div>

> Furthermore, `->` is more expressive in switch statements (ones that return a value) because if you use `:`, you have to write `yield &lt;value>`.

Respectfully disagree on _more expressive_: Every computational construct that can be expressed using `->` can also be expressed using `:`.\* Besides, having a multi-statement block within a `switch` which returns a value requires the usage of `yield &lt;value>` for both `->` and `:`. (Though TIL you can `yield` in a `switch` block, thank you!)

\* Which would mean I was incorrect on my own "strictly more expressive" claim as well.

From this I realize there is an ambiguity in terms of style requirements, specifically for multi-statement blocks that `yield`, coming from two different viewpoints:
1. Multi-statement blocks should use the `:` style (snippet 1, under "The *switch* statement should have the following form:").
2. Case arms in a `switch` expression that produces a value should use the `->` style (snippet 3).

For now I believe as long as you stick to either convention it should (reasonably) be acceptable.

P.S. Minor nitpick: Constructs that return a value are *expressions*, not statements \:p
</panel>

</panel>


