@BrianLim35

 I think the first issue about code integrity with regards to program behaviour is rather testable. Assuming one has infinite tokens the AI can be instructed to write tests for every single class/methods. One just needs to verify that the tests generated are sound and captures all/most edge cases. Even without the AI, the human still has to write/review test cases. So this isnt really any extra work.

For the issue on code organisation one can give detailed instructions on how the repo should be organised, or if one is feeling particularly unmotivated can get the AI to suggest possible organisation structures, letting the human choose from a list.

This is not to say the human should just take a completely hands off approach. The human should still be familiar with the repo structure. At the end of the day is it faster to write 10 000 lines of code or to understand a a repo with 10 000 lines ?  I'm incline to think that the latter is the more convenient choice. 
