<div slot="header">

**3 :fas-comment:**
</div>

At first I was also confused by this, but maybe the example below can help to clear up the confusion a little bit. 

'''java
class Student ``{`` //class 1
    void printCourseName(Course course) ``{``
        System.out.println(course.getName());
    ``}``
``}``

class Course ``{`` //class 2
    private Student student;

    Course(Student student) ``{``
        this.student = student;
    ``}``
``}``
'''
`Class2` has an association with `Class1`, meaning it keeps a reference to a `Class1` object. At the same time, `Class1` separately depends on `Class2`, for example by using it as a method parameter.

The note that _"the dependency is not implied by the association"_ means that the association from `Class2` to `Class1` does not automatically create the reverse dependency. `Class1` must independently use `Class2` for that dashed dependency to exist.

</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/118#issuecomment-5482373799" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**4 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I do agree with @kaseyho with the point about a line without arrowheads meaning that navigability is unspecified.

> Anyways, here's an example for an association between 2 classes without having a reference to the other:
> 
> class Man ``{``
>     // no reference to Dog
> ``}``
> 
> class Dog ``{``
>     // no reference to Man
> ``}``
> 
> class PetShop ``{``
>     void sellDog(Man man, Dog dog) ``{``
>         // sells this dog to this man
>     ``}``
> ``}``

But I’m a bit unsure about the `Man` and `Dog` example. Since neither `Man` nor `Dog` stores a reference to the other, and they only appear together as parameters in` PetShop.sellDog()`, wouldn’t that represent a dependency involving `PetShop` rather than an association directly between Man and Dog?

My current understanding is that an association usually represents a more "structural relationship" between objects, often reflected by one class storing a reference to the other. Would `Man --- Dog` still be considered an association in this case even though the relationship is not stored anywhere?



</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/119#issuecomment-5491842684" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**5 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I have decided to prompt AI and ask it what it thinks. Honestly the remarks are quite close to what @yxlyx and @h0nh0nbaguette are talking about. I shall quote the following remarks as such:
> UML multiplicities describe constraints on valid model states. 
> Temporary intermediate states during an operation can violate them, 
> as long as those states are not externally observable and the invariant is restored when the operation completes.

**Personal take:** 
I think that the issue is not simply whether the method temporarily violates the multiplicity, but whether that intermediate state is exposed beyond the method’s abstraction boundary. If the method restores the invariant before control returns and no external code can observe the temporary state, I think that the abstraction will still hold.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/127#issuecomment-5497292788" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**6 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Correct me if I am wrong. 

My view is that the model specifies the intended structure and constraints of the system, while the code is responsible for enforcing those constraints. 

Therefore, a multiplicity of 1 expresses that a `Student` should always have exactly one `Professor`, but Java does not enforce this automatically. It is up to the programmer to ensure, through constructors, setters, and other methods, that the reference is never `null`.
</panel>

