<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/119#issuecomment-5491911034" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**3 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I never think of this multiplicity 1 this way before but I agree with the points above. Multiplicity constraints typically apply to stable or observable states, not transient internal states. That said, the move operation must be atomic with rollback. if the add fails after the remove, the task must revert to its original list. Otherwise, the next observable state would violate the multiplicity.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/118#issuecomment-5492139277" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**4 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Hey @DaRealTristan @zikiai , just wanted to share my understanding too. I think a line without arrowheads means navigability is unspecified, not that there's definitely no reference, so the association can be implemented in different ways and doesn't strictly require Man or Dog to hold each other's references. 

I agree with @zikiai that PetShop.sellDog() feels more like a temporary dependency from PetShop to Man/Dog, but @kaseyho Kaseyho's idea still works if we use a more persistent setup like a PetOwnership class that holds references to both Man and Dog

'''java
class Man ``{``
    private String name;
    // no reference to Dog
``}``

class Dog ``{``
    private String name;
    // no reference to Man
``}``

// Association class – holds the relationship
class PetOwnership ``{``
    private Man owner;
    private Dog pet;
    private LocalDate startDate;
``}``
'''

In this case, Man and Dog have no direct references to each other, yet the domain association clearly exists, so drawing Man --- Dog would still be valid. Hope this helps! 
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/117#issuecomment-5493162073" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**5 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I fully agree with @darrenori, abstraction is about hiding how something is done, not hiding what is done. processInput() gives no guarantee at all so the caller has no idea what processing means without diving into the implementation, which defeats the purpose of abstraction. Regarding your point about future changes, if you later need to do more than just remove whitespace, that means your method is now doing multiple things. In that case, removeWhiteSpaceFromInput() should stick to its single responsibility, and you can introduce a higher-level method like processInput() that orchestrates multiple steps.
</panel>

</panel>


