### 25. LIM ..NDAN `@Brendan-Lim` (5 posts) 
</div>


<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/112#issuecomment-5482009158" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**1 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I think this question is more than just "AI can make mistakes". AI can generate code that looks correctly, but actually inconsistent with what the user was trying to implement with the rest of the project. It might invent methods/APIs that don't exist, or fully misunderstand a class' responsibility if JavaDoc comments were missing, or solve one issue to pass a testcase but unknowingly fail another.

Therefore project context becomes even more important when using AI to code, as well as endlessly testing, reviewing code and doing QA before accepting changes to push out the final code.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/116#issuecomment-5482143736" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**2 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Gnanes99 covered this point well but to add on to his point, I think one way to differentiate the two is to look at whether there's already an existing product which has behaviours and constraints you are expected to maintain, instead of whether there is already existing code.

Having starting code doesn't necessarily make a project brownfield if that code is mainly skeleton. We are still creating the actual product, deciding the behaviour and features, and setting the limitations ourselves. I would consider that greenfield. This also seems to fit the iP where some starting code and skeleton was provided but we are still expected to personalise it and build it ourselves.

For tP, we have an existing address book and limitations are given to maintain it as an address book, only allowing us to add features to it but not morph to another whole different product. That seems closer to brownfield development.

I would think the distinction is whether you are expected to build a whole new product (greenfield) or expected to maintain and improve the existing product (brownfield), and not looking at whether code is provided or not.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/117#issuecomment-5482350609" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**3 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I believe we should not deliberately choose a vague name just in case the method changes later. If the responsibility of that previous method changes significantly enough for its old name to become inaccurate, that might itself is a sign that the method's expected behaviours has changed and an entire change in method is appropriate.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/124#issuecomment-5494017723" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**4 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I also struggled with understanding the dependency arrow at the start, but one way that helped me understand the direction is to relate the dependency arrow back to code.

If class A uses class B, for example:

```
class A ``{``
    void doSomething(B b) ``{``
        b.run();
    ``}``
``}``
```

then changes to B could potentially affect A, because A is using B. So I would read this as A depends on B, and the dependency arrow goes:

A --> B

Perhaps the teaching team could consider adding an example like this in the notes for better understanding.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/127#issuecomment-5504110537" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**5 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I agree with @zikiai that the variable declaration alone does not enforce the UML multiplicity. Java only guarantees the type of the reference via declaration, but not that the multiplicity or whether it is non-null. The implementation in code must then enforce this constraint when creating constructors and setters after the engineer has understood the UML diagram. 

If the association should not change, the field can also be made final to ensure so.
</panel>

</panel>


<panel type="info"  collapsed>
<div slot="header">

### 26. DARR..N EN `@darrenori` (4 posts) <span class=text-warning>:octicon-eye:</span>
</div>


<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/119" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**1. :fas-info-circle: Must UML multiplicities hold at every moment? What if it dosent in certain situations?**
</div>

I'm quite new to this, but I had a scenario in mind. Suppose a Task must belong to exactly one TaskList, represented by a multiplicity of 1. When moving the task between lists, the program might first remove it from the original list before adding it to the new one. In that moment, the task belongs to no list and violates the multiplicity.

Would this intermediate state mean the implementation does not conform to the class diagram, or are multiplicities only expected to hold before and after an operation is completed?
</panel>

