<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/73#issuecomment-5436863525" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**3 :fas-comment:**
</div>

yup same here. I would treat the old entries as invalid, but handle them gracefully by skipping only the affected task and continuing to load the rest of the file. One reason not to automatically convert values such as is that their meaning depends on when the user originally entered them. If that reference date was not saved, converting the string during a later start-up could produce the wrong deadline.



</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/117#issuecomment-5488418030" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**4 :fas-comment:**
</div>

> According to the naming conventions mentioned in [W4.6d](https://nus-cs2103-ay2627-s1.github.io/website/book/codeQuality/nameWell/intermediate/useNameExplain/), `removeWhiteSpaceFromInput` should be used in lieu of `processInput`.
> 
> Isn't this antithetical to the principle of abstraction, though? `processInput` as a name hides the actual operations taking place from the calling class. If it turns out in the future that further processing is required beyond removing whitespace, `removeWhiteSpaceFromInput` would not be an accurate name. Wouldn't `processInput` allow a smoother change in that case since the function API remains the same?

This is tricky! But I feel that abstraction should hide how an operation is performed, not hide what the operation promises to do.
removeWhiteSpaceFromInput() tells the caller the method’s contract while still hiding whether whitespace is removed using a loop, regex, or another technique. By contrast, processInput() is so broad that the caller cannot infer its purpose without reading the implementation preciselly.
</panel>

</panel>


