<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/57" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**1. :fas-info-circle: Tips for tracking technical debt**
</div>

While doing the iP, I've realized that in some situations, I create an unideal solution for an increment first. This is because the more robust solution sometimes requires another component to be implemented first.

For example, I know that parsing the commands within `main` is unideal. However, making classes for the command requires a huge refactoring for the Chatbot class to be more stateful. So, instead I'll leave a `TODO` comment to fix it later. In VSCode, this shows up on the Problems tab.

<img width="325" height="44" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/a16403b4-0b86-4c1e-aa78-8d4b543b9e49" />

This is useful to track technical debt that needs to be fixed later. Does anyone have other ways to track this?

(PS: I'm not sure whether this should be a Tip or a Question)




</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/69#issuecomment-5435658011" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**2 :fas-comment:**
</div>

> I think a small `switch` inside `Parser` is still fine if it only creates the correct `Command` object. The main problem is when the switch also parses arguments, modifies tasks, handles storage, etc.
> 
> Also, most commands probably need a new instance for each input, e.g. `TodoCommand("read book")`, so a map of command factories may work better than reusing the same `Command` objects.

Yep, I agree with this idea. I split the responsibility between the `Parser` and `Command`, where the `Parser` would just intepret the texts and create the relevant `Command` object, while the `Command` would be the one that executes the command. So, we can always make sure that the `Command` is typeful and has valid objects for the flags. Errors in the execution would never be type errors.

However, I'm still quite unsure whether there is a fix to the long method body. For example, in my iP, I added a `filter` command that accepts extra flags like `/type` (the type of task), and a few other optional flags like `/due-by` for deadlines or `/at` for events. 

So, when parsing the `filter` command, we still need to check whether the optional `/due-by` flag or the `/at` flag exists. Hence, the parsing for a command will still be long if there are many optional flags. 
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/69#issuecomment-5435679796" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**3 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I'm also curious about how we can use HashMaps to do this. I'm aware that hashmaps can help map the command (or the first word of the command) with a function. But, the function would still be the one responsible for parsing the arguments. The first `parse` function wouldn't be long, but the function to parse the arguments would still be quite long.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/69#issuecomment-5435895358" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**4 :fas-comment:**
</div>

> If I’m understanding what u said correctly, I splice my String input into args and parse it into my abstract functions! This way, I can access what the user typed via indexes.

So a line like `/by Today` would get parsed as a Map where the index "by" has value "Today"? So, the function would get the map of all the arguments. Hmm that's interesting, maybe I should do that too.
</panel>

</panel>


