<div slot="header">

**2 :fas-comment:**
</div>

In my experience, short conversational prompts add up fast since each new message re-processes the entire conversation history, so token cost compounds even if individual prompts are small. Personally, I use `/compact` often to shrink my context window to save on tokens while retaining the knowledge of my previous messages. Starting a separate chat also saves on tokens but you do lose the context from your previous chat. To circumvent this, you can ask Codex to update your `AGENTS.md` file.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/91#issuecomment-5450091854" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**3 :fas-comment:**
</div>

In my experience, UML came up mostly in initial design discussions/whiteboarding, but the diagrams was maintained long-term were using the C4 model, not full UML. We used UML more as a reference tool for early designs than a documentation standard.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/126#issuecomment-5493274327" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**4 :fas-comment:**
</div>

To me, it seems like you want to imitate the behaviour of `Scanner` using JavaFX GUI (correct me if I am wrong). You can look into using a `BlockingQueue&lt;String>` to replicate the blocking nature of `Scanner.nextLine()`.

However, this comes with many annoying problems like running this queue asynchronously from your UI to prevent your entire UI from being blocked. Therefore, I do feel like the provided code snippet is the cleanest implementation.

I would love to know if anybody has a cleaner way to emulate the `Scanner` class in JavaFX!
</panel>

</panel>


