On branch main
nothing to commit, working tree clean

git merge feature/payments
Merge made by the 'ort' strategy.
 payments.html   | 23 +++++++++++++++++++++++
 src/payments.js |  4 ++++
 2 files changed, 27 insertions(+)
 create mode 100644 payments.html
 create mode 100644 src/payments.js

git commit -m "Merge feature/payments into main"
On branch main
nothing to commit, working tree clean

gitmastery verify
Status: Completed
```
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/119#issuecomment-5490230792" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**3 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I agree, though I’d add that putting `remove()` and `add()` in one function doesn’t necessarily make the move atomic. Another thread, callback, or error could still expose the task while it belongs to no list. The move may need a transaction, lock, or rollback so that it either completes fully or leaves the task in its original list.

If being unassigned is a valid observable state, then the multiplicity should probably be `0..1`. Otherwise, `1` means every stable, observable state should have exactly one list.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/134#issuecomment-5511109091" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**4 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I think this is just called a dependency (where `Foo<String>` is dependent on `Foo<T>`). 

<img width="621" height="134" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/8fd9af79-ef1c-4f60-8f02-ebd955a6996d" />

`A dependency exists when one component relies on another to do its job. Dependencies have a direction, and that direction matters more than almost anything else in an architecture.`
</panel>

</panel>


