<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/49#issuecomment-5386008255" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**2 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Correct me if I'm wrong, but from my understanding, token usage can accumulate easily because the model is repeatedly sent the context containing previous conversation history.

Just thought I could suggest some ways I reduce token usage in my own projects (other than just choosing a cheaper model):

1. Use a different session for each new feature. I believe this was mentioned in one of the earlier levels

2. Make use of the context compaction feature.
This is the "/compact" command in most AI harnesses. Using this summarises the conversation history, reducing the context being fed to the LLM every time the harness sends a request. For longer tasks I also ask the llm to generate a plan and execute the plan without past context. While information is lost, the plan should have sufficient information to handover to a new session (else u could edit the plan manually or work with the ai to improve the plan).

3. Some AI harnesses might have a subagent feature. This allows the main agent to delegate smaller tasks to other subagents, so it doesnt pollute the context of the main thread. This is similar to the plan feature I mentioned earlier, except that after the subagent is done, the main agent can takeover and carry on.
EDIT: I think rayyan is right, subagents can drain quite a bit of tokens. Might only be useful in some cases like letting the subagent explore a bunch of files and report back to your main agent.

4. In large refactoring with no change in logic, u might also be able to use LLMs to write a script to automate the change, instead of reading all the files and then editing them directly. 
I also know that IntelliJ has its own MCP, which might be useful in some refactoring tasks (they have tools for the AI agent to move code from one class to another or rename a class, variable, etc, which automatically moves/renames the references as well)
But when using these MCPs, u can uncheck the useless tools to reduce the context used also

official plugin: https://plugins.jetbrains.com/plugin/26071-mcp-server
unofficial extension that adds missing features: https://plugins.jetbrains.com/plugin/29174-ide-index-mcp-server

Hope this is helpful. U can probably try other AI harnesses like OpenCode as well to make use of the free limits while waiting for your codex and gemini cooldown
OpenCode can also link to your Codex and Gemini account, so you can try it if u want one harness for different accounts
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/69#issuecomment-5434015785" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**3 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Haven't tested if this works, but I am considering using something like this to dynamically load the relevant Command class and execute it: `Class.forName(className)` (Some string transformation probably can be done to convert `command` to PascalCase for `className`)

Then the object can be casted to a parent class or interface at runtime, with each class overriding method that executes the command.

I think this might be a good solution to reduce the need to both create the class and add the switch case as the only thing we will need to change for each new command is the class's Java file itself.
</panel>

</panel>


