<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/49#issuecomment-5386182168" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**2 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Here's what usually drains a lot of tokens for me using claude, (and it should be relevant to your case too adapted to Codex)

1.  Spawning subagents costs more in total tokens than doing it inline. Each subagent gets its own fresh context window, so it re-greps and re-reads things your main thread already knows. The tradeoff is that it keeps your main conversation clean, so it's worth it for genuinely noisy side quests and not much else.

2. Another thing is your project context (like .md files), not sure if applicable to yours, but if your project context is a large file, then everytime it loads, it consumes tokens as well 

3. Plan using higher model (for eg Opus for claude) and execute using a lower model (Like sonnet or haiku for trivial task in claude)

4. Also, try to start a different thread if possible for each feature, what i do is have a skill like /handoff, which summarises key context so i can resume in another thread (like a memory)

You can consider GitHub Student Developer Pack. Verified students get free Copilot (student tier, not Pro , it's a credit pool with auto model selection), which is at least a separate quota pool from Codex/Antigravity.
</panel>

