<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/69#issuecomment-5434170189" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**2 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I think command classes are useful, but reflection may be overkill here. A short `switch` or a `Map&lt;String, CommandFactory>` can handle parsing, while each `Command` class contains its own execution logic.

The map may still be long, but it is just a list of mappings rather than complex control flow. `Class.forName()` also introduces runtime errors if a class is renamed or has the wrong constructor. For an iP with a fixed set of commands, an explicit switch or map seems simpler and easier to test.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/96#issuecomment-5461525139" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**3 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I took a look at the error file and it seems like an architecture mismatch. The JavaFX files are built for `x86_64`, but your Java is trying to run them on `arm64`.

Could you run `uname -m` and `java -XshowSettings:properties -version 2>&1 | grep os.arch`? If they show ARM64/aarch64, you may need an ARM-compatible version of the JAR, or use an x86_64 JDK through Rosetta. Installing another ARM JDK probably won’t fix the mismatch.
</panel>

</panel>


