<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/76#issuecomment-5447674080" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**1 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I ran into this choice too and ended up using composition. My TaskList keeps a private ArrayList&lt;Task> and only exposes the operations my app needs. Inheritance seemed reasonable at first because a task list sounds like a list of tasks. However, extending ArrayList would also expose methods like clear, set, and addAll. With composition, I can control what other classes are allowed to do and add validation later if needed. Inheritance can work for the current IP, but composition made more sense for my implementation.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/119#issuecomment-5489823512" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**2 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I believe that it only applies to the observable state, not temporary states. meaning that if the move is treated as a singular operation, and as long as mid move the task is not able to be accessed, it is not a violation.

This also means that when performing the move, errors need to be taken into account. For example, your program's `move` function calls `remove(task)` followed by `add(task)`, but then `add(task)` fails and the program does not revert the task back to belonging to its original TaskList. In this case, multiplicity is violated.
</panel>

</panel>


