### 59. LIM ..OVAN `@Matcha1atte1` (2 posts) 
</div>


<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/84#issuecomment-5447981574" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**1 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Here's some Codex specific rules that I typically follow:
- **Run `/compact` at natural checkpoints** (e.g. once a subtask is done) instead of waiting for auto-compaction to kick in. It summarizes the conversation so far and frees up context, but doing it manually at a clean breakpoint preserves the useful decisions/state better than letting it auto-trigger mid-task.
- **Drop reasoning effort for simple subtasks.** You mentioned you're on setting:Mid - for boilerplate-y or mechanical changes (renames, simple CRUD, styling tweaks) dropping to Low burns way fewer tokens per turn since it reasons less before acting. Save Mid/High for the actual tricky logic.
- **Use `codex exec` for one-shot, well-specified tasks** instead of the interactive chat mode when you already know exactly what you want becos it skips the conversational back-and-forth entirely and just executes.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/117#issuecomment-5481299700" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**2 :fas-comment:**
</div>

To add on, I think the distinction here is also on specific / vague naming. A name like `sanitizeInput` is still abstract, but it promises something (input ends up sanitized) whereas `processInput` promises nothing since you have to read the body to know what it does.
</panel>

</panel>


