### 62. SPEN..HENG `@spencercdz` (2 posts) 
</div>


<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/126#issuecomment-5511708465" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**1 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I think the event handler is a reasonable boundary here. handleUserInput() does not need to contain the chatbot logic. It can act as the GUI controller: read the text field, call a method on the chatbot/application layer, then render the returned response.

For example, the flow can be:
`GUI event handler -&gt; Duke/Logic.process(input) -&gt; response -&gt; GUI renders response`

That keeps the GUI responsible for JavaFX controls and display, while Duke or Logic owns parsing commands, updating tasks, and producing a response. You do not need to simulate two servers or use a BlockingQueue unless you have a separate long-running task to move off the JavaFX UI thread.

As the project grows, you can extract the handler’s work into a small controller method, but the callback itself is still the normal way for JavaFX to pass a user action into the application.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/126#issuecomment-5511760072" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**2 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I think the event handler is a reasonable boundary here. It does not need to contain the chatbot logic; it can simply read the text field, call an application-facing method such as `duke.getResponse(input)`, then render the returned response.

That gives a straightforward flow:

`GUI event handler -&gt; Duke/Logic.process(input) -&gt; response -&gt; GUI renders response`

The GUI remains responsible for JavaFX controls and display, while `Duke` or `Logic` owns parsing commands, updating tasks, and producing the response. You do not need to simulate two servers or use a `BlockingQueue` for a normal button-click interaction.

If a command later performs genuinely long-running work, that specific work can run in a JavaFX `Task` so the UI stays responsive. For typical iP commands, keeping the handler thin is the simplest and most maintainable design.
</panel>

</panel>


