### 64. HU X..YUAN `@HXY070103` (2 posts) 
</div>


<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/75#issuecomment-5467173729" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**1 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I have been thinking about an alternative way to organize command parsing.
My idea is to keep CommandParser responsible only for separating the command word from its arguments and dispatching the input to the appropriate Command:

public Command parse(String input, TaskList tasks)
        throws Exception ``{``
    String[] inputParts = input.trim().split("\\s+", 2);
    CommandType commandType = CommandType.from(inputParts[0]);
    String argument = inputParts.length == 2
            ? inputParts[1].trim()
            : "";

    return switch (commandType) ``{``
    case FIND -&gt; new FindCommand(argument);
    case MARK -&gt; new MarkCommand(argument, tasks);
    case DELETE -&gt; new DeleteCommand(argument, tasks);
    case LIST -&gt; new ListCommand(argument);
    case BYE -&gt; new ExitCommand(argument);
    // Other commands...
    ``}``;
``}``

The abstract Command class can define a common argument-parsing operation:

public abstract class Command ``{``
    protected abstract void parseArgument(String argument)
            throws Exception;

    public abstract void execute(TaskList tasks, Ui ui, Storage storage)
            throws Exception;
``}``

Each concrete command then overrides parseArgument() because it understands its own argument format best:

public final class FindCommand extends Command ``{``
    private String keyword;

    public FindCommand(String argument) throws Exception ``{``
        parseArgument(argument);
    ``}``

    @Override
    protected void parseArgument(String argument)
            throws Exception ``{``
        if (argument.isBlank()) ``{``
            throw new Exception(
                    "The keyword of find command cannot be empty.");
        ``}``

        keyword = argument.trim();
    ``}``

    @Override
    public void execute(TaskList tasks, Ui ui, Storage storage) ``{``
        ui.showResponse(tasks.listTasks(keyword));
    ``}``
``}``

With this design, CommandParser remains the single source of command dispatch, so a switch or map is still necessary. However, the important difference is that the central parser no longer contains the detailed parsing rules of every command. Adding a command mainly involves creating its Command class and adding one dispatch entry, rather than extending a large parser with more validation and parsing methods.
</panel>

