Hi, 
I would like to clarify further on the requirements for Level 8, at Level 4 where initial support for dates was added, the requirements stated:
_At this point, dates/times can be treated as strings; there is no need to convert them to actual dates/times._

_Example_:
_deadline do homework /by no idea :-p_
    ____________________________________________________________
     _Got it. I've added this task:
       [D][ ] do homework (by: no idea :-p)
     Now you have 6 tasks in the list._
    _______________________________________________________________

For Level 8, what is the expected behaviour for dates not in the yyyy-mm-dd format (e.g., 2019-10-15)? Are we still expected to provide support these inputs (e.g. /by no idea :-p_) or are we only accepting dates in the yyyy-mm-dd format? Thank you.
</panel>

<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/76#issuecomment-5436836724" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**2 :fas-comment:**
</div>

This is an interesting problem that I also encountered. Personally, I used composition to model the taskList as a List&lt;Task> field, as I felt it gave me better encapsulation and more control over what methods I wanted to expose through taskList. For instance, the taskList would only have 3-4 specific methods available that other classes can call to edit the taskList instead of exposing all methods from ArrayList.

That being said, it does make sense that TaskList is-a list of tasks, and hence the argument for using inheritance is also logical, interested to hear further opinions on both approaches!
</panel>

</panel>


