<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/125" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**1. :fas-info-circle: Should tP teams use `.gitattributes` to standardise line endings?**
</div>

Hey guys while reading about Git collaboration across different operating systems, I came across the difference between Windows CRLF and macOS/Linux LF line endings.

Was just curious suppose a tP team has members using different operating systems. A teammate makes a small change to a Java file, but GitHub displays most or all of the file as modified because its line endings were converted. This could make the pull request harder to review and create unnecessary merge conflicts.

Would it be advisable for tP teams to add a repository-level `.gitattributes` file to standardise line endings? For example:

'''gitattributes
* text=auto
*.java text eol=lf
*.gradle text eol=lf
'''

Could this configuration conflict with IntelliJ, Gradle, teammates’ existing Git settings, or any CS2103 grading and progress-tracking tools?

</panel>

