@damithc 

Hi prof, I just wanted to flag some work I did on the Gitmastery CLI from within this thread that I think goes beyond the usual community engagement scope of the module.

Over the course of a few days I:
1. Identified a real bug in the gitmastery application affecting other users as well (https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/4#issuecomment-5274778462)
2. Independently followed the stack trace, identified the root cause and suggested a possible solution (all within this issue thread)
3. Proceeded to then fork and clone the source code of the gitmastery cli application (https://github.com/angus-yxz/app), make changes locally, write E2E tests, push code to my fork and then open a PR (https://github.com/git-mastery/app/pull/151) which was then subsequently merged into the codebase solving the issue (https://github.com/git-mastery/app/commit/d798b0c74e8a0dd3b20ec237629b501a349f7876)

This fix is now live for all current and future students using the tool. Given this, I would like to request that credit be counted towards the 5 extra marks of community engagement section of the course (given that in my opinion going this far as to both documenting an issue _and_ having a real fix be merged into code used by every other student goes far beyond simply raising or discussing issues in a forum).

Additionally if there's room, I think I have also demonstrated core git competencies (forking, branching, staging, committing, PRs, working with live remote repos) that the gitmastery portion of this course assesses, so I would also like to ask whether exemption from the rest (or part) of the gitmastery course with credit counted towards CP3108A (which requires completing a portion of gitmastery) would also be possible.

I'd also be happy to walk you through the entire process of the implementation and fix (including demonstration of core git competencies) in person if you need verification.
</panel>

</panel>


