<panel type="info"  collapsed>
<div slot="header">

### 106. JAVI.. LIM `@JavierLimZH` (1 posts) 
</div>


<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/76#issuecomment-5448049049" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**1 :fas-comment:**
</div>

> technically, TaskList is a List of Tasks

I'm not sure I completely agree here, since a "List" is a very precise definition (see the API), and as others have pointed out, can inherit other unintended methods.

Another consideration is that, even if you decide that a TaskList is-a List of Tasks, that is distinct from it being an *ArrayList* of Tasks. It might not be correct to inherit from ArrayList directly. The additional methods you write can operate on *any* List, and so inheriting from ArrayList destroys this property. 

This may seem contrived, but consider: ArrayList is not thread-safe, and so we way wish to use a slower thread-safe List in some areas, while still being able to use the fast ArrayList when this is not needed. 
Afaik, there is no clean way to maintain this property without composition. 

While you can implement an AbstractTaskList which implements these methods in terms of the un-implemented list interface methods, you then have no easy way to wire up the concrete list implementation
With composition, we can do something like TaskList() defaulting to TaskList(new ArrayList()), while we can still do TaskList(new ThreadSafeList()) when needed.
</panel>

</panel>


<panel type="info"  collapsed>
<div slot="header">

### 107. NGUI..ISON `@nje14` (1 posts) 
</div>


<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/133" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**1. :fas-info-circle: 💡Auto-format your code with the three key presses**
</div>

Most IDEs provide a way to auto-format your code with the correct indentation and spacing

On IntelliJ, select a code block (`Ctrl` + `A`) and run

`Ctrl` + `Alt` + `L`

See the [IntelliJ Documentation for it](https://www.jetbrains.com/help/idea/reformat-and-rearrange-code.html)

If you're on VSCode, this defaults to

`Alt` + `Shift` + `F`

Note that this is **separate** from the Checkstyle document as documented in the [checkstyle tutorial](https://se-education.org/guides/tutorials/checkstyle.html)

The autoformatter can only fix indentation and spacing issues

It does **not** fix naming, access modifier and other naming standard violations that otherwise require rewriting code


</panel>

</panel>


