<panel type="info"  collapsed>
<div slot="header">

### 126. WANG..AHUA `@jiahw4ng` (1 posts) <span class=text-warning>:octicon-eye:</span>
</div>


<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/87#issuecomment-5453454082" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**1 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I think the Java coding standard gives the impression that `->` should be used when the body of the case is a single method call or value, and `:` otherwise (if the logic of the case is more complex and requires more than one line). Furthermore, `->` is more expressive in switch _statements_ (ones that return a value) because if you use `:`, you have to write `yield &lt;value>`. 

Also note that switch expressions written using `->` have no fall-through behaviour, while you need `break` if you use `:`.

So I think it really depends on your use case. but for the IP chatbot you probably will write a single method to handle each command, so `->` keeps things more concise.
</panel>

</panel>


<panel type="info"  collapsed>
<div slot="header">

