<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/124#issuecomment-5492915080" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**1 :fas-comment:**
</div>

I actually found the direction quite intuitive once I read it as the phrase “depends on”... which we hear quite often.
So like if AA depends on BB, I think of it as “AA depends on what?” (BB)... and so the arrow points towards BB.

That said, I can definitely see how it could be confusing when one first learns UML, especially since arrows in different kinds of diagrams we have seen before can mean different things. It might help if the notes included a simple sentence like “the arrow points to the class being depended on,” alongside an example. That would make it much easier to remember rather than just having to memorise the notation.
</panel>

</panel>


