### 146. QUAK..OMMY `@tommyquak` (1 posts) 
</div>


<panel  popup-url="https://github.com/NUS-CS2103-AY2627-S1/forum/issues/75#issuecomment-5473172816" expanded>
<div slot="header">

**1 :fas-comment:**
</div>

Really cool thread, learnt a lot reading this. My iP is on the other side of the spectrum, one central Parser class with static parseTodo / parseDeadline etc methods, plus an enum for the command words and a switch to dispatch.

Adding on to the switch discussion, one thing I found while reading up is that if you switch over the enum with a switch expression (java 17+ like mentioned above) and leave out the default, the compiler forces you to cover every command, so adding a new enum value won't even compile until you handle it. Mine is still an old style switch with a default so I don't get that yet, but thinking of converting it. With the map you get the easier extension instead, just register your parser and done, plus less merge conflicts like @Aster0 said. So both sides come with their own kind of safety net and you just pick which one you value more.

@HXY070103 I like this a lot, each command knowing its own argument format feels very SLAP friendly, the central parser stays short and every command's grammar has an obvious home. Might steal this for my own iP haha
</panel>

</panel>


